  "handoff": {
    "summary": "General word-prime dense matrices now canonically own generated FLINT nmod_mat resources for construction, mutation, structural operations, vector products, algorithms, formatting, and serialization. Prime fields below 256 retain packed UInt64 storage and exact Modular<float> FFLAS/FFPACK acceleration; p >= 256 uses retained nmod_mat after a direct crossover audit showed it matches SageMath and the legacy oracle while Modular<double> is modulus-sensitive. The Modular<double> declarations remain explicit and differentially tested through Givaro's exact range without becoming canonical storage. Linux x64 public benchmarks use exact decimal-string digests and compare compiled/dynamic Sage.js, retained legacy N-API, and SageMath; lifecycle fuzzing covers 200 dynamic and 500 sanitizer rounds.",
    "risks": [
      "The generated nmod resource is currently Node-gated because native FLINT ulong width and resource lowering have not yet been established for Wasm32; portable execution retains the existing fallback.",
      "This lane validated and benchmarked on linux-x64; macOS arm64 and linux-arm64 remain required follow-up hosts.",
      "Public wrapper construction/copy overhead makes 160x160 p=65537 rank and RREF a few tenths of a millisecond slower than direct retained-resource calls even though the underlying generated resource algorithms match the legacy and SageMath backends."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Run the checked-in benchmark receipt on macOS arm64 and linux-arm64 and retain host-specific crossover history.",
      "Measure and, if warranted, reduce generated resource-wrapper overhead without changing canonical ownership or adding operation-specific handwritten N-API.",
      "Establish an honest Wasm32 lowering for generated nmod resources or retain a capability-tested portable representation fallback."
    ]
  }
}
